---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProvenanceBlock from '../components/ProvenanceBlock.astro';
import { patentProvenance } from '../lib/data';
---

<Base
  title="Governed Semantic Layer — Jack Kelly"
  description="A documented semantic layer over governed patent data, with natural language access and deliberate refusal behavior when a question cannot be answered honestly."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Project Two</p>
  <h1>Governed Semantic Layer</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Anyone can wire a chat input to a language model. The differentiator is the governed
    layer underneath it: documented entities, metrics with explicit formulas, allowed
    joins stated up front, and refusal when a question falls outside what the data can
    honestly answer.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-limit">
    <span class="callout-label">The claim being made</span>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      The layer is the artifact. The chat box is the least interesting part of this
      project, and the page is built so the layer stays at least as visible as the
      answers it produces.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why the layer exists</h2>

  <p>
    Point a language model at raw patent data and it produces confident nonsense. Not
    because the model is poor, but because the data carries knowledge that is nowhere in
    the schema:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Assignee names are not identities.</strong> "Google LLC", "Google Inc.",
      and "GOOGLE LLC" are three strings and one company. A model asked to count patents
      by company will happily produce three rows, and it will be wrong in a way that
      looks entirely plausible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Grant dates are not filing dates.</strong> Asked about activity "this
      year", a naive query answers with grants — which reflect decisions made years
      earlier. The number returned is real and the answer is misleading.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sector membership is a judgment call, not a column.</strong> The
      CPC-to-practice mapping is authored. A model cannot infer it, and if it guesses,
      the guess is invisible in the output.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Some counts must never be summed.</strong> Sectors deliberately overlap,
      so adding them double-counts. Nothing in the raw data signals that constraint.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Encoding these facts so a machine can act on them correctly is the work this project
    is actually about. The layer is where that encoding lives.
  </p>

  <h2>The layer definition</h2>

  <div class="callout callout-limit">
    <span class="callout-label">Not yet built</span>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Entities, dimensions, metric formulas, and allowed relationships are defined over
      the processed patent dataset, which does not exist yet. Writing the definitions
      against guessed column names would produce a layer that documents nothing real, so
      this section waits for the data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Refusal behavior</h2>

  <p>
    A system that answers everything is a system that lies sometimes. The demonstration
    includes questions the layer correctly declines, because declining is the governance
    behavior worth showing. Categories it should refuse:
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Question type</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it is refused</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Anything about pending applications</td>
          <td>The dataset covers granted patents only. The answer is not in the data.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Which companies need outside counsel</td>
          <td>
            Patent activity is a demand proxy. It carries no information about existing
            counsel relationships, and answering would fabricate a conclusion.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Revenue, headcount, or company size</td>
          <td>Not present in this data, and not joinable from it without another source.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Totals summed across sectors</td>
          <td>Sectors overlap by design, so the sum double-counts.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Filing activity in the last few quarters</td>
          <td>
            Grant lag makes recent periods structurally incomplete. A number could be
            returned; it would mislead.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Shipping this safely</h2>

  <p>
    A public static site cannot safely hold a model API key — client-side keys are
    extractable. The approach here is a curated gallery of pre-run questions, each
    showing its real generated query and real answer, computed ahead of time and cached,
    clearly labeled as pre-run, with the source code linked. It costs nothing, cannot be
    abused, and is honest about what it is. A live version runs locally for interviews.
  </p>

  <h2>Data provenance</h2>

  <ProvenanceBlock
    record={patentProvenance}
    pendingLabel="USPTO patent tables — not yet acquired"
    pendingNote="The layer is defined over the processed patent dataset. Until the source tables are acquired and inspected, there is no dataset to govern and no provenance record to show."
  />
</Base>
